course: dictionary
lesson: 4918
title: Immigrant: a word for people who move
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An immigrant is a person who has come to live in a new country from another country. Look for the clue «from» in the sentences. Immigrants come from one place and settle in another. «Millions of immigrants came to America from Europe, Asia and Latin America.» These people moved to America from many different places.
explain: Immigrant can also be an adjective, a word that describes a noun. «Immigrant families» are families who have moved to a new country. To check, see if you can put the word people or families after it. If you can, it is probably an adjective.
explain: People become immigrants for many reasons. Some move for work, some for safety, some to be with family, and some for a better life. «After living legally in a country for some years and passing tests, immigrants can become citizens with the right to vote.» So an immigrant can become a citizen later, if they follow the rules.
explain: Many classmates and their families are immigrants, so this word is about real people you may know. Use it with respect, and remember that a person is more than the word for where they came from.
fact: An immigrant is a person who has come to live in a new country from another country.
fact: Immigrant can also describe people, as in «immigrant families».
fact: Countries such as the USA, Canada, Australia, New Zealand, the UK and Ireland have many immigrants.
fact: «The ukulele grew out of small Portuguese guitars brought to Hawaii by immigrants from Madeira in the 1870s and 1880s.»
fact: Immigrants move for work, safety, family or a better life.
exercise: «Millions of immigrants came to America from Europe, Asia and Latin America.» What is an immigrant? | A kind of musical instrument | *A person who has come to live in a new country from another country | A tall statue on an island | A person who has lived in the same town all their life
exercise: Look at «immigrant families». In this phrase, what kind of word is immigrant? | *An adjective, describing the families | A verb, an action word | A noun, the name of a person | An adverb, telling how something is done
exercise: «After living legally in a country for some years and passing tests, immigrants can become citizens with the right to vote.» What can immigrants become after this? | *Citizens with the right to vote | Tourists who visit for one week | Statues holding up a torch | Immigrants from Madeira
test: Which reason could make someone become an immigrant? | *To find work or to be safe | To win a prize at school | To read a book about history | To visit a museum for one afternoon
test: «Canada welcomes immigrants from all over the world.» What does welcomes mean here? | Closes its doors to | Forgets about | Sends away from | *Opens its doors to and is friendly towards
exam: Which sentence uses immigrant as an adjective? | Immigrants sailed past the Statue of Liberty. | *The immigrant families cooked a meal from their homeland together. | She is an immigrant who arrived from Poland last year. | An immigrant is someone who moves to a new country.
